# Low Poly Colorizer — Godot import notes

Generated by Low Poly Colorizer (LPC).
<https://github.com/wasdcat/low-poly-colorizer> — a WASDCAT Games project.

This folder holds the shaders, materials, and **two lookup textures** for the
LPC look in Godot 4 (4.4 or newer): `{{prefix}}palette.png` (one sRGB color per
palette cell, decoded by the shaders via `source_color`) and
`{{prefix}}preset_lut.png` (raw preset data). The shaders read single texels by
cell, so Godot's default texture import (lossy VRAM compression, mipmaps, Fix
Alpha Border) would corrupt them.

`{{prefix}}palette_cheatsheet.png` is the palette with its name, group, row and column
names — for people, to look up cells. Its `.import` file tells Godot to skip
it (not imported, not exported with the game); open it in any image viewer.

**Nothing to set up:** every export writes the matching `.import` files next
to the PNGs, so Godot imports them correctly right away. Do not change their
import settings — the next export restores them anyway:

- **Compress → Mode = Lossless**, **Detect 3D → Compress To = Disabled** —
  no VRAM compression, which would shift every palette color.
- **Mipmaps → Generate = Off** — a data texture must never blend across cells.
- **Process → Fix Alpha Border = Off** — `{{prefix}}preset_lut.png` packs
  roughness / metallic / clearcoat / emission into R/G/B/A, so alpha is not
  transparency; the default would overwrite every preset with `emission = 0`.

If colors or presets ever look wrong after copying the files around by hand,
make sure the `.import` files came along, or simply export again.

## Stable UIDs

Every exported file carries a fixed UID (in its `.uid` / `.import` file, or in
the `.tres` header). It depends only on the file name, so every export writes
the same UIDs — scenes and resources referencing these files by UID keep
working across re-exports, fresh checkouts and other machines.

## Assigning the material

The geometry with its per-face data reaches Godot separately via the `.blend`
import (the palette cell / preset position travel in the mesh's UV maps). On
the imported mesh, set the surface's **Material Override** to
`{{prefix}}multicolor.tres`. For meshes **not** painted in the add-on, use
`{{prefix}}singlecolor.tres` and set its per-instance shader parameters in the
`MeshInstance3D` inspector. There, `lpc_palette_cell_x` is a drop-down of the
palette's columns by name ("Group · Column") and `lpc_preset_position` one of
the preset names; what is stored are the numbers (column, list position).

## Named looks — `{{prefix}}singlecolor_resource.gd`

`{{prefix}}singlecolor.tres` is meant to be **shared**. Every mesh using it can
still look different, because its palette cell, preset and emission are
*instance* uniforms: they live on the `MeshInstance3D`, not on the material.

`{{prefix}}singlecolor_resource.gd` wraps those four values as a resource, so a
look can be authored once, saved as a `.tres` ("Gold", "SignalRed", …) and
reused by name:

```gdscript
@export var stop: {{prefix_pascal}}SinglecolorResource
@export var go: {{prefix_pascal}}SinglecolorResource
@export var lamp: MeshInstance3D

func set_clear(clear: bool) -> void:
	(go if clear else stop).apply_to(lamp)
```

The lamp's **Material Override** is still yours to set, as described above —
the resource only carries values, it holds no reference to the material and so
does not care where in the project the exported files live.

It also knows the palette's actual colors — see the next section.

The script also carries a `Preset` enum generated from the add-on's preset
list, so you write `Preset.EMISSION` instead of looking the number up in the
legend comment inside `{{prefix}}singlecolor.gdshader`. Godot prettifies the
member names in the inspector dropdown, so a preset called “Signal Red” shows up
as `Signal Red` there. Names are folded to identifiers on the way in and
non-ASCII is stripped (“Grün” becomes `GRUN`), so the exact spelling only
survives in that legend comment.

Three things worth knowing:

- **`class_name` is project-global in Godot.** Two LPC exports living in one
  project need two different prefixes, otherwise Godot reports a duplicate
  class name.
- **Instance uniforms apply to a whole `GeometryInstance3D`**, not to a single
  surface. Two parts of one `MeshInstance3D` cannot show two different looks —
  split them into separate nodes if they must.
- **Resources are references.** All your signals share the same two `.tres`
  files. That is usually what you want; if you *modify* one at runtime instead
  of just applying it, every user of it changes too.

## Palette colors only — `{{prefix}}palette_color_resource.gd`

Sometimes only the color matters: a decal, a `Label3D`, a UI swatch, a light.
`{{prefix_pascal}}PaletteColorResource` holds nothing but a palette cell
(`palette_cell_x` / `palette_cell_y`) — no preset, no emission — and
`get_color()` returns its color. Unlike a plain `Color` it stays tied to the
palette: re-export with changed palette settings and it takes on the new color
of its cell, like the painted meshes do.

`{{prefix_pascal}}SinglecolorResource` **extends** it, so an export typed as
the palette color accepts a full look as well:

```gdscript
@export var paint: {{prefix_pascal}}PaletteColorResource  # takes either kind

func _ready() -> void:
	$Label3D.modulate = paint.get_color()
```

`color_at(x, y)` returns any cell's color without a resource in hand:

```gdscript
$Lamp.light_color = {{prefix_pascal}}PaletteColorResource.color_at(2, 3)
```

The colors are not recomputed in GDScript and not read back from the texture:
the add-on writes every cell's color into the script as the `PALETTE` constant,
from the same computation that fills `{{prefix}}palette.png`. So they follow
every palette setting (groups, columns, hue shift, warmth), and no texture or
import setting is involved. The shader samples the 8-bit PNG, so the two differ
by less than 1/255 — use the result for display, not for equality comparisons.
The palette's parameters are there as `PALETTE_*` constants for your own code to
build on. Re-export after changing the palette to refresh all of it.

In the Inspector, `palette_cell_x` is a drop-down of the palette's columns by
name ("Nature · Moss"); the resource still saves the number. The groups and
names are also there for code: `PALETTE_GROUPS` (name, first column, column
count per group) and `PALETTE_COLUMN_NAMES` (one label per column).

**Reordering columns shifts the numbers.** If you add, remove or move palette
columns in the add-on, a saved `palette_cell_x` names a different column after
the next export — the same as with preset positions. The add-on remaps the
painted meshes; your `.tres` files keep their numbers, so check them after such
a change.
